hello and welcome to the spring 2011  edition of Rochford district matters the  information paper for the people of  Rochford district in this edition are  articles on a new wedding venue in the  area news of your council tax  information on welfare reforms how to  prepare for an emergency events around  the district and much more but to start  and with the 2012 Olympics in mind  here's an invitation to support our  stars  rochford District Council's leisure team  shop at my local and active Rochford are  working together on an exciting new  project to support our local athletes  there are many talented athletes in the  district already competing on the  international stage who need additional  support to be able to continue to excel  at the highest level local businesses  are being invited to join star at my  local which is supporting talented  athletes in Rockford as silver or bronze  sponsors sponsorship runs from april  2011 to march 2012 businesses that are  part of the shop at my local scheme have  the opportunity to support and sponsor  two very special athletes sailing  champion lucy Hodges and badminton star  Rebecca Terry the current Rochford  District sports personality of the year  Lucy won gold at both the 2010 blind  Nationals and ifds blind match race in  international championships Rebecca is  the Rochford district and Essex young  sports personality of the year and one  of the youngest players to represent  Essex at senior level active Rochford  the community active network for the  Rochford district has kick-started the  sponsorship by becoming a gold sponsor  with 1,000 pounds if you want to get  involved contact mark oldest sports  development officer on 0 1 702 31 8 121  or mark dot oldest let's al do us at  Rochford WK  telling us what you think rochford  District Council is looking for  volunteers who would like to give us  their views on the services we provide  it's a chance for you to take part and  tell us exactly what you think about a  range of issues that will help us take  decisions about desert services if  you've got an opinion and want to tell  us about it then here's how you can get  involved the group is made up of  residents who volunteer to express their  views on the council's proposals and  local services the information provided  helps the council to make its decisions  the group seeks to involve all members  of the community to ensure that the  views expressed are as representative as  possible in order for us to get the  information we need you will be asked to  fill in questionnaires take part in  telephone surveys and participate in  focus groups when signing up to take  part in the group you will be asked to  highlight any particular areas of  interest this is to ensure your views  are taken into account when relevant  consultations come up you will be  approached to get involved in  consultation throughout the year you  don't have to respond every time we just  ask that you get involved at least once  a year members are selected to get  involved according to the type of  consultation sometimes every member of  the group will be called upon sometimes  it may be only 20 people questionnaires  shouldn't take longer than 20 to 30  minutes but focus groups will take  longer the group gives you the chance to  express your opinions to the people that  make decisions you can help to represent  the views of the whole community by  bringing your ideas and viewpoints to  the group being part of the group will  be a valuable experience and is an  opportunity to give something back to  your community the consultation  exercises help the council to understand  what residents think about the issues  and as a result can help to make to help  to influence  making members will receive a regular  newsletter explaining how their views  have helped in the decision-making  program process you can sign up to the  group on the council's website at www k  forward slash have your say or you can  contact andrew lowing community planning  officer on 0170 231 8061 or andrew  lowing that's lowi ng at Rochford gov UK  raley Rochford and district association  for voluntary services ravs has a wide  range of support on offer across the  community in the Rochford district ravs  is once again running its popular laptop  courses which aim to help the over-50s  learn computing skills in a relaxed  atmosphere laptop can help you with the  beginners computer course and home  visits among the skills the course  teaches are how to visit websites that  you've seen on television how to send  emails with photos attached how to  transfer photos from cameras to  computers and print them out how to  learn about internet shopping revs is  also offering free courses aimed at  helping people aged 19 and over who are  unemployed hoping to return to work or  who do not have any formal  qualifications to find employment or  further training courses include CV  writing interview skills online job  searching and basic computer skills  they're being delivered by ravs under  contract with Essex County Council adult  community learning and funded by the  skills funding agency the sessions take  place on Saturday and Monday mornings  until the end of June 2011 excluding  public and bank holidays from ten a.m.  to 1pm at Rochford parish council parish  rooms West Street Rochford SS 41 AAS and  if you want to learn laptop contact ravs  on 01 268 double 727 96 or email them  ravs that's double our AVS at ravs org  dot u K and ravs news in brief over  Christmas ravs once again organized its  annual families in need appeal to  support local families and children in  the Rockford district with food parcels  and presence more than 70 parcels of  food and goodies were distributed to  families across the district  to help make Christmas special for them  at least two hundred and ten children  were helped by the appeal this year  perhaps was helped in the annual appeal  with continued generous support from  local churches rotary and lions clubs  and donations from other organizations  and individuals  the rayleigh windmill is now licensed  for weddings naming ceremonies and  renewal of vows the windmill was built  in 1809 and thrived in the early 19th  century set in the heart of historic  rayleigh the windmill has been  sympathetically refurbished to allow the  continued enjoyment and use of the venue  it is located next to the sensory garden  and historic Raley mount owned and  managed by the National Trust a perfect  backdrop for your pictures Raley has a  host of venues for receptions and other  items to help make your day extra  special including florists jewelers  formal wedding attire and decorations  with seating for up to 20 guests Raley  windmill offers an intimate venue for  your civil wedding civil partnership  naming ceremony or renewal of vows  several packages are available including  the option to hold your reception at the  mill Arts &amp; Events Center adjacent to  the windmill the services of a dedicated  event coordinator are at your disposal  to ensure that your special day runs  perfectly to arrange a viewing or to  discuss your specific requirements  please contact watch for District  Council on 0 1 702 318 120 or 0 17025  463 double 6 extension 3619 or visit w  WR OTE govt UK forward slash weddings  once again the rayleigh windmill has  achieved the accreditation of quality  assured visitor attraction from visit  england this is the second year in a row  that the windmill has received this  recognition and a call for volunteers  the windmill would not be as successful  or popular as it is today without the  support and generosity of its volunteers  we are always looking for more people to  become involved in the windmill for more  information on events at the windmill  details on how to get involved in  volunteering or exhibitions at the  windmill contact the lazer team on 0170  25 463 double six or email leisure at  Rochford  dot uk'
 

